  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1227 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nHello Fall, is that finally you? After what has seemed like a\nnever-ending summer across the Mid-South, we finally have some\ntrue fall-like conditions today behind a cold front. Temperatures\ncurrently span the 60s for much of the area, with a few locations\nstill in the 50s. While the main, surface cold front has cleared\nour area, we are beginning to see a few post-frontal showers\ndevelop west of I-55. Conditions are generally dry enough at the\nsurface that much of this activity likely will not reach the\nsurface, but some light rain cannot necessarily be ruled out as\nit moves from northwest to southeast across the area. Outside of\nthis, gusty winds will continue with a fairly steep pressure\ngradient associated with the upper-level trough passing through. \nMuch of the area is reporting 10 to 20 mph sustained winds with\noccasional wind gusts upwards of 30 mph. This pressure gradient\nwill linger into Saturday as the parent low pressure system moves\noff to the east, so expect some occasional 20 to 25 mph wind\ngusts to continue. High temperatures on Saturday will remain well\nbelow normal, with highs spanning the 60s.\n\nBy Sunday and into next week, a weak upper-level ridge will begin\nto develop over the region with dry conditions persisting for the\nforeseeable future. LREF guidance remains in fairly good\nagreement with this dry pattern, with a general warming pattern\nthrough the week. Sunday morning will start off with some of the\ncoolest temperatures we have seen so far this season, with many\nin the upper 30s to low 40s. For locations generally north of I-\n40, temperatures may come close to Frost Advisory criteria.\nLatest NBM guidance does have about a 40 to 50% chance of\nlocations falling below 37 degrees, so this will be something to\nwatch with upcoming forecast packages. Despite the chilly start,\ntemperatures will warm back into the 60s to near 70 by Sunday\nafternoon. Into the rest of next week, high temperatures will\ngradually rise a few degrees each day, likely reaching the upper\n70s to low 80s by the middle of the week.",
